Type 1 Hypervisor (Bare-Metal)

┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│  [ Guest VM 1 ]    [ Guest VM 2 ]    │  <-- Virtual Machines (Windows, Linux)
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│         Type 1 Hypervisor            │  <-- Directly controls hardware (No host OS)
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│       Physical Hardware (CPU/RAM)    │  <-- Bare-metal server
└──────────────────────────────────────┘

What It Means
"Bare-Metal": It is installed directly onto the empty hard drive of a physical server. It replaces the standard operating system.

No Middleman: When the machine boots, the hypervisor loads first. It speaks directly to the CPU, memory, and network cards without needing Windows or macOS underneath.

Why It Is "Fast, Efficient, and for Professional Environments"
Maximum Performance: There are no background desktop apps, graphical interfaces, or system bloat consuming RAM and CPU cycles. Nearly 100% of the hardware power is dedicated to running VMs.

High Security & Stability: Because there is no general-purpose host OS underneath, there are fewer software bugs and a much smaller attack surface. If one VM crashes or gets infected, the others keep running untouched.

Where It Is Used: Data centers, enterprise server racks, and cloud infrastructure (AWS EC2, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure).

Industry Examples: VMware ESXi, Proxmox VE, Microsoft Hyper-V Server, KVM.

. Type 2 Hypervisor (Hosted)

┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│  [ Guest VM 1 ]    [ Guest VM 2 ]    │  <-- Virtual Machines (Kali Linux, Ubuntu)
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│         Type 2 Hypervisor            │  <-- Application (VirtualBox, VMware Fusion)
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│        Host OS (Windows / macOS)     │  <-- Everyday operating system
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│       Physical Hardware (CPU/RAM)    │  <-- Your laptop/PC

What It Means
"Hosted": It runs as a regular desktop program inside your existing operating system (the "Host OS"), just like a web browser or media player.

